.TH SETUPFS 4
.SH NAME
setupfs \- what a phone in radio range may do to a board with no network
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount
.B {setupfs
[
.BI -w " wififile"
] [
.BI -c " ctlfile"
] [
.BI -n " ipifcdir"
] [
.BI -l " logfile"
] ...
.B }
.B /mnt/setup
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Setupfs
serves the namespace a board exports over Bluetooth when it has no
network to be reached by
.RI ( bt9p (4),
.BR "announce le9p" ):
enough to put the board on a network, or to find out why it is not on
one, and nothing else. Radio range is a weaker boundary than a wire, so
the tree is the whole of what a paired phone can touch, and access
control is by placement: the listener exports this tree and never the
root.
.IP
.EX
mount {setupfs} /mnt/setup
echo pairable on > /net/bt/ctl
echo advertise on > /net/bt/ctl
listen 'bt!*!le9p' {export /mnt/setup}
.EE
.PP
The files:
.TP
.B wifi
Read, it names the network the board is configured to join and says
whether a passphrase is set, and never gives the passphrase:
.RS
.IP
.EX
essid My Network
password set
.EE
.RE
.IP
or
.B unconfigured
if the card has no configuration. A write replaces the configuration.
It must be the whole of one in a single write, in the card file's own
syntax, both lines present:
.RS
.IP
.EX
essid My Network
password the passphrase, rest of line
.EE
.RE
.IP
so that a configuration that is refused is refused on the write that
offered it. An essid is 1 to 32 bytes and a WPA passphrase 8 to 63. The
new file is written beside the old one and renamed over it: a power cut
leaves one or the other. It takes effect at the next boot. The
passphrase is not logged, and is cleared from memory once written.
.TP
.B net
Read only: the status of each IP interface, one per line, under the
interface's number, as
.B /net/ipifc
gives it.
.TP
.B log
Read only: each log named with
.BR -l ,
under a line naming it;
.B (nothing)
for one that is empty or absent. By default the Wi-Fi supplicant's,
which is where a join that failed says why.
.TP
.B ctl
Write only.
.B reboot
restarts the board, and
.B tryboot
boots the candidate kernel once. Nothing else is accepted.
.PP
Writes to
.B wifi
and
.B ctl
are recorded on
.B /mnt/audit/log
if it is there, without the passphrase.
.PP
The options name what stands behind each file, for a board laid out
differently and for the test:
.TP
.BI -w " file"
The Wi-Fi configuration; default
.BR /n/dos/wifi .
.TP
.BI -c " file"
Where
.B reboot
and
.B tryboot
are written; default
.BR /dev/sysctl .
.TP
.BI -n " dir"
The IP interfaces; default
.BR /net/ipifc .
.TP
.BI -l " file"
A log to serve. May be repeated; default
.BR /tmp/wpa.log .
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/setupfs.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bt9p (4),
.IR export (4),
.IR listen (1)
.SH BUGS
A configuration is one network. Several, with a policy for choosing
among them, is a separate proposal.
